over the weekend mark german's power on  newsletter gave us a huge amount of  clarity in terms of what's going to  happen with the imac later this year  he said that this year the transition to  apple silicon will shift into high gear  with several new mac models based on the  following processors a new m2 chip last  year's m1 pro and m1 max chips and new  super powered versions of the m1 max and  to give you some more context on what  those super power chips will be  apple is said to be taking multiple m1  max dies and combining them together  using a die to die interconnect in sets  of either two or four dies to  essentially create m1 max duo and m1 max  quadro super chips and that first m1 max  duo chip was exactly what we were  expecting to go into the imac pro for  various reasons which i'm gonna show and  explain in this video including internal  software leaks but unfortunately mark  german has just made it completely clear  that he doesn't believe the imac pro is  gonna be getting this new super powered  m1 max duo chip which is extremely  disappointing so before i explain  exactly what he said over this weekend  and why it's so important to understand  i want to first get into all the reasons  why we thought it made sense for the  imac pro to get this new high-powered  chip with both leaks and common sense so  let's get right into that last year in  november after we already got our hands  on the new m1 pro and m1 max macbook  pros ross young confirmed that the new  imac pro will be coming with a 27-inch  mini led display with 120hz pro motion  technology and that right there was a  big deal because it hinted to us that  apple was about to go all out on this  new imac pro since implementing that  technology would be pretty expensive and  then shortly after that a software  developer named hector martin who was  currently working on bringing linux to  apple silicon max found something  groundbreaking  in terms of these new m1 max dies  apparently the irq controller in the m1  max is very clearly engineered with a  currently unused second half for a  second die he even said it's technically  a second set of registers that are  currently all idle and going even  further the irq controller only has  support for up to two dies not four and  because of all this it basically showed  that apple had already put everything in  place in terms of the software side of  mac os to connect two m1 max dies but  not yet for four of them so this led me  to believe that apple planned to release  an m1 max duo mac before the release of  the next macos software version which  would mean that we should see a new m1  max duo imac pro in the spring before  apple announces the new mac os in june  like they always do but what really got  me believing in an m1 max duo imac pro  was a couple of very good points made by  some people on twitter first of all the  apple tree made a great point about the  limitations of the regular m1 max chip  specifically that it only supports up to  64 gigs of unified memory which is  already half the amount compared to  apple's current intel 27-inch imac from  2020. so if apple wanted to keep that  same 128 gig ram option around the best  way to do that would be to introduce the  m1 max duo chip with literally double  the number of ram chip slots and then  going further he pointed out that the m1  max 32 core gpu is limited to around  10.4 t flops of performance which is  definitely lacking compared to other  desktop gpus like for example only  matching up to the raw performance of  the rtx 2080 or the rx vega 56 which is  literally built into the 2017  imac pro that i'm using right now so the  idea that apple would release a new imac  pro with a 32 core m1 max gpu that's  basically only as powerful as a 5 year  old base model imac pro sounds  ridiculous so i thought they would  absolutely need to give the new model an  m1 max duo chip with a 64 core gpu and  then perhaps the weirdest part of all is  that the current 27 inch intel imac has  two chip options that are actually  faster than the m1 max chip in terms of  raw cpu performance and keep in mind  that these are 14 nanometer desktop  chips from 2020 and if we take a look at  intel's new alder lake chips even the  new i5 12600k is about 23 faster in  terms of single core and 39 faster in  terms of multi-core performance in  cinebench r23 and then the flagship i9  12 900 k which uses over four times more  power is actually 119  faster in terms of cinebench so with  that said i again assumed that apple  needs to pack in a 20 core m1 max duo  chip to compete but unfortunately mark  german just released a new report over  the weekend that hints at the most  disappointing imac pro we could ever  imagine apparently he believes that the  larger imac pro will come with m1 pro  and m1 max chip options that's it he  even confirmed this further by saying  this and with the m1 pro and m1 max  chips already on the market i don't  believe the imac pro launch is too  distant either while we'll get new max  in march i'm told apple is already  gearing up for another round of mac  releases around may or june and that's  exactly when ross young is personally  expecting apple to reveal the new imac  pro so this claim by mark german could  come as an absolute shock because many  of us were expecting a much more  powerful chip coming in the apple  silicon imac pro especially if apple  wants to give it the pro title and we  even had some reports from dylan dkt  that apple is working on a 12 chord [ __ ]  for the imac pro instead but mark still  believes that we'll only be getting up  to the m1 max so with all of that  disappointing news out of the way let's  actually try to make sense of the idea  of the imac pro not getting anything  more than an m1 max chip the first point  to make is that even though the raw  performance numbers for the m1 max look  disappointing compared to the  competition the real world performance  is actually really good for example the  affinity photo benchmark which was  optimized for apple silicon shows the 32  core m1 max as being the fastest gpu in  the world for that editing app even  beating out high-end desktop gpus and in  terms of video editing performance the  m1 max outperformed our 15 000 mac pro  in various tests even the ones we didn't  think it could pull ahead in like 8k  canon raw and for things like  programming we were surprised to see  that the m1 max was quite a bit faster  than the mac pro  so even though the raw performance  numbers aren't that impressive the real  world performance is actually more than  enough for most people out there so that  right there could be a good reason for  apple not to bother with putting an m1  max duo into the imac pro since the  regular m1 max is already enough for  almost everyone out there and to make  this point very clear max's friend owns  a full-time wedding videography business  with multiple employees and they all use  m1 max no not the new m1 max macbooks  but literally 24 inch m1 imacs and he  himself ended up replacing his trashcan  2013 mac pro with an m1 macbook air  instead of getting a desktop 2019 mac  pro and he's never been happier in fact  he was initially planning on using the  m1 macbook air until the new 16-inch m1  max macbook pro was released but he's  been so happy with his macbook air that  he decided to keep it since it's fast  enough for all of his video editing  needs so the point that i'm making is  that real people and real businesses are  using apple's original m1 chip with  great success and that's not even the m1  max chip which is many times faster  especially for high-end workflows so in  reality most people out there probably  do not need anything more than the m1  max chip and they definitely don't need  the crazy 20 core m1 max duo chip and i  think a lot of us have fallen under  benchmark sickness with many of us  wanting our machines to be the fastest  in terms of benchmarks for bragging  rights even if it ends up being absolute  overkill for the tasks that we actually  need it for so while i would really  really like apple to pack in an m1 max  duo chip into the imac pro i have to  admit that for my workflow of using  photoshop in final cut pro for editing  the regular 10 core m1 max chip in the  16 inch macbook pro is probably already  overkill for my needs and going even  further if apple gave the imac pro an m1  max duo it would likely be so fast that  it would cannibalize the sales of their  upcoming high-end mac pro desktop  machine since that 20 core duo chip  would literally be fast enough for 95  of the common use cases out there so  what apple could do is limit the imac  pro to just the m1 max chip perhaps  upping the clock speeds a bit to make it  a bit more powerful and then offer  people who actually need much more power  the high-end mac pro desktop machine and  the major benefit to this is that it  would allow them to lower the base price  of the imac pro to make it more  affordable to the average consumer  likely around two thousand dollars at  the base price instead of the previous  imac pro which started at a massive five  thousand dollars and then on top of that  i already concluded in a different video  that the m1 max duo chip wouldn't be  able to fit within the imac pro's chin  but if the best option is the regular m1  max chip then they can easily fit it  within the chin and keep the design as  thin as they'd like to make it without  having to go all out on the cooling and  just like mark german said earlier doing  this would allow them to get this new  imac pro out earlier without having to  wait for this new duo chip or anything  like that so in the grand scheme of  things the reality is that apple could  easily get away with releasing an m1 pro  and m1 max imac pro and people would  still be buying it left and right  including ourselves but hopefully mark  german is wrong about all of this and  hopefully we actually do get a more  powerful chat and with that said let me  know what you think down in the comment  section below about all of these leaks  and rumors and if you enjoyed this video  go ahead and click the circle above to  subscribe for more videos like this one  and check out one of those two right  over there thanks for watching and we'll  see you in the next video  [Music]
 

